593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186552596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186552609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186552616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186552618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186552622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1085 "![](images/07/07a6945f159c5472eab5cce55d00cf8b361c50ea45dadd261a6a69c3304aa647.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "566b96aa-b9fb-5bb6-bd2d-dc9c552adfd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552400 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186552544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552439 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552439 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552439 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552439 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552439 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51d9f041-304f-5c22-9ed6-3b3659033e6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552439 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=5{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nFrom the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=0,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.$ So, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}) & =5{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)^{3} \\\\  & =0+6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552441 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b9c1b56-216e-5690-b417-9acbe40876e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552441 #edb/ref 1074 "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $-1$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$  the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552447 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552447 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}Cf(x)=8,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "684140b2-affb-5ccb-83ee-320705bb77fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552447 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=-1.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}Cf(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot (-1) & =8 \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552462 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552462 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552462 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552462 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552462 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b122753-4256-5b6b-bc1d-003f930bcebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552462 #edb/ref 1074 "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$ It is impossible to draw the function in a neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552464 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "825d48d1-753b-5c3d-88ac-771966b26aa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552464 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552495 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552495 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b69f893c-224f-5a62-ac7c-a87ffc7746ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552495 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=-1$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=-1$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552519 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552519 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552519 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552519 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed765a29-aa16-50e2-b510-c72034032862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552519 #edb/ref 1074 "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552521 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552521 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552521 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552521 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552521 #edb/ref 1071 "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n$g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552521 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f30773ae-09b1-517b-85fe-c0701b657a78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552521 #edb/ref 1074 "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552544 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552544 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "217dd86a-e5c9-5e13-8d12-918c91954a89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552544 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47cc6d9a-f608-5cdf-87ea-8b6b430ef0e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552589 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552589 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a50f47ae-5b45-529d-9ee2-a14e82bcf521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552589 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552593 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552593 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b1e40b46-de09-596e-8b86-6632ee88a63c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552593 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552596 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552596 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba9fc067-856a-57fe-a6ff-ee2103364a79" #edb/ref 175